<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Daunians</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 318 BCE (approx.) · capital Arpi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Iapygian people of northern Apulia, known for their carved stone stelae and painted pottery, who allied with Rome against the Samnites.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Daunians were the northernmost of the Iapygian peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>, settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tavoliere-delle-puglie">Tavoliere</a> plain and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gargano">Gargano</a> from the early 1st millennium BCE. Greek legend made the hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diomedes">Diomedes</a>, returning from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>, the founder of Arpi and other towns, a story the Daunians themselves seem to have adopted.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Daunian society was organised in large settlements such as Arpi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canusium">Canusium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salapia">Salapia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sipontum">Sipontum</a>, with chieftains whose wealth appears in their tombs. Between the 7th and 6th centuries BCE they set up hundreds of anthropomorphic stone stelae near Sipontum, carved with weapons, ornaments and scenes of daily life. Their matt-painted pottery is distinctive. They farmed the plain, raised horses and traded across the Adriatic.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samnite-wars">Samnite Wars</a> the Daunian towns sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> records alliances in 318 BCE, and Rome founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucera">Luceria</a> in 314 BCE. Arpi went over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a> in 216 BCE and was punished when retaken. The towns became Roman municipia after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a>, and Daunian identity faded into Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/daunians.jpg" alt="Daunians" width="320" height="267"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jug_Daunia_550-400BC_MAN_Madrid.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 318 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Arpi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Daunian polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Messapic (Iapygian)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Daunii</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-559&amp;focus=daunians" aria-label="Daunians on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5059 699 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Daunians lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5379 955L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5379 955h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5379 955h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Arpi. <a href="/atlas?year=-559&amp;focus=daunians">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

